00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:13.150
area as well.

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:16.380
Now, as we hit
play, the app also

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:19.440
provide information
for that user

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:22.530
so that the user understands
what it is they need to do.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:24.405
So from an AC
inspection perspective,

00:38:24.405 --> 00:38:25.905
there's usually
three things you are

00:38:25.905 --> 00:38:28.350
going to look at, the
condenser, the pressure that's

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.832
within the system, but
also check the fixtures

00:38:30.832 --> 00:38:32.040
and fittings around the hose.

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:34.570
Is there any leakage
happening in that space?

00:38:34.570 --> 00:38:36.820
So essentially, the user
is given basic information,

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:38.362
and then they're
guided through this.

00:38:38.362 --> 00:38:40.960
So if we click play,
start inspection,

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:43.930
they'll be told,
obviously, where they

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:45.850
need to focus their attention.

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:48.730
They'll scan the asset, so
this is using the camera

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:52.850
to pull back visual information,
check against our model,

00:38:52.850 --> 00:38:55.870
and then it pulls back the
information around what

00:38:55.870 --> 00:38:57.000
it is they need to do.

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:59.590
So at this point, we've called
out the three inspection areas.

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:02.020
Now, for different
vehicles, different cars,

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:04.180
you will have different
areas to have the hose

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:06.100
or have the condenser
unit, and this

00:39:06.100 --> 00:39:09.100
allows them to click in to see
what it is they need to work on

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.020
and the steps they need
to carry out as well.

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:14.530
So it's removing the end of
job checklist activities,

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:16.420
and it's focusing
that user on what

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:19.170
they need to be doing when
they need to be doing it.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:21.090
This can get as
complex as you want

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:29.670
it to be dependent on what
asset you're working on.

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:32.290
So it is really powerful.

00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:36.180
And then obviously, the
information is pulled through,

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:39.240
and that passes
through to the database

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:41.340
so that we can have positive
feedback around what

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:44.100
was completed, whether there
was actually a fault in it,

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:46.680
reinforcement with reason
codes so our model can

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:49.040
learn as we go.

00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:52.940
So that's the demo from
the AR perspective.

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:54.870
And this is where we're
hoping that it works.

00:39:54.870 --> 00:39:56.630
And we can go next.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:58.435
Again, the value of
why we're doing this.

00:39:58.435 --> 00:40:00.560
So the value of why we're
doing this for our client

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:04.220
is to help them reduce
the maintenance burden

00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:06.800
and improve efficiency from
a maintenance perspective,

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:09.140
so go and maintain
those assets before they

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:12.560
fail so you don't have to
spend more time and effort when

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:14.750
those assets are out
of commission later on

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:17.300
down the line.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:19.170
Improve the uptime
for those assets.

00:40:19.170 --> 00:40:21.917
So if we can get to a vehicle
and do a small repair as

00:40:21.917 --> 00:40:23.750
opposed to having to
do a large replacement,

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:26.880
it's then available to
be used by the parties.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.550
And reducing that capital
cost, so you do not

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:31.560
want to be going out
and repairing AC units

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:34.740
as frequently as
you currently do.

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:37.410
You want to be able to maintain
those units to a higher

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:40.530
level of efficiency so that
they last a lot longer as well.

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:47.700
So the key takeaways, and I know
you're all probably wondering

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:51.210
so what and how much all of
this would factor in from a data

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:52.920
processing perspective.

00:40:52.920 --> 00:40:55.260
I'll pass it to Andy there.

